1b:["$","p","1",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"A due date tells you when something ends, not when to start. Large assignments do not get done because they are remembered; they get done because pieces of them are scheduled like appointments. Working backwards from the deadline turns one intimidating task into four ordinary ones."}]
1c:["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-primary-200 bg-primary-50/50 p-6","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"block text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-primary-700 mb-2","children":"Worked example"}],["$","h4",null,{"className":"text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1","children":"Scheduling a paper due in three weeks"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-5","children":"Turn one deadline into a plan you can actually follow."}],["$","ol",null,{"className":"space-y-4","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":1}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Write the deadline, then work backwards in pieces, not in hours."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"'Spend six hours on the paper' is not schedulable. 'Choose a topic and find three sources' is a task you can finish and tick off, which means you will start it."}]]}]]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":2}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Set the draft deadline three days before the real one."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"The gap is for revision, and revision is what separates a C paper from a B. It is also your buffer — a printer failure or a shift change on the last day becomes an inconvenience rather than a disaster."}]]}]]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":3}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Put each piece in your calendar at a specific time, in a specific place."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"'Sunday 10am at the library, find three sources' happens. 'Work on the paper sometime this week' does not. Naming the place matters more than people expect."}]]}]]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":4}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Schedule the pieces around your work shifts first, not after them."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"Your job is fixed and your study time is not, so the study time has to fit around it. Building a plan that assumes free evenings you do not have is how a plan gets abandoned in week two."}]]}]]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-5 pt-4 border-t border-primary-200 text-gray-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Result: "}],"Four scheduled tasks instead of one deadline, with three days of slack before it."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-gray-900 font-medium","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","children":"🔑 "}],"Schedule the pieces, put them somewhere specific, and build in a buffer you expect to use."]}]]}]
1d:["$","div","3",{"className":"p-4 rounded-xl border-l-4 text-gray-800 leading-relaxed bg-yellow-50 border-yellow-400","children":[false,"There will be a point where you genuinely cannot do everything. That is a decision, and making it deliberately is far better than letting it happen. Talk to an advisor before the withdrawal deadline — a W on a transcript is survivable and does not affect your GPA; an F is neither. Advisors would much rather have this conversation in week eight than see the result in week fifteen."]}]
1e:["$","$L22","4",{"block":{"type":"knowledge-check","question":"You are failing one of four courses and cannot catch up. It is week nine, and the withdrawal deadline is week eleven. What is the best move?","options":["Keep going and hope the final exam rescues the grade","Talk to an advisor about withdrawing, so the term ends with a W rather than an F","Stop attending and let the grade fall where it falls","Withdraw from all four courses and restart next term"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A withdrawal appears on the transcript but does not affect GPA; a failing grade does both. Speaking to an advisor first matters because withdrawal can affect financial aid and full-time status, and they can tell you how it applies to your situation before you act.","optionRationales":["Sometimes a final does rescue a grade, but hoping is not a plan and the deadline passes either way.","Correct. Talk to an advisor before the deadline and take the W deliberately.","Stopping without withdrawing produces the F anyway, plus a possible financial aid problem.","Withdrawing from everything is a much larger step with aid and enrollment consequences. Deal with the one course that is failing."]}}]
1f:["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-12","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-[40px] sm:leading-[1.05]","children":"Challenge"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-3 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-soft","children":"Apply what you've learned in this lesson."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-md border border-gray-200 p-8","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-6","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"Build the schedule you will actually use."}],["$","ol","1",{"start":"$undefined","className":"list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-gray-700 marker:font-semibold marker:text-primary-600","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"Map one week hour by hour: work shifts, class, travel, family commitments, and sleep. Total what is left."}],["$","li","1",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"Compare that remaining figure against credits × 2, which is your required study time. Write down what you will change if it does not fit."}],["$","li","2",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"Take one real upcoming assignment and break it into four scheduled pieces with dates and locations."}],["$","li","3",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"Find the withdrawal deadline for your term and put it in your calendar now, while nothing is wrong."}]]}]]}]}]]}]
